{{!-- The route step: the book's regional maps, made tappable, over its travel table.

      Two halves, and only the first is optional. The MAP needs art the GM imported from their own
      rulebooks (it is gitignored and stripped from releases, so it can never ship), while the
      DESTINATION LIST below is always rendered — it is the map's legend and, in a world with no
      art, the whole screen. That is what makes the no-map case cost nothing extra.

      Every hotspot's left/top is a percentage precomputed in ExpeditionDialog._buildJourney
      (data/travel-times.js spotPercent), so Handlebars does no arithmetic and nothing here has to
      measure the rendered image. --}}
{{!-- `is-needing-start` is what tells the destination list below that its rows are about to answer
      "where do they set out from?" rather than "where are they bound?" — the state a trip is in
      after a right-click has peeled it back past its own start. It rides the ROOT because both the
      map and the list are inside it and both answer that click. See ExpeditionDialog `needsStart`,
      which is GM-only: a player cannot write the trip, so their rows must not offer to. --}}
<div class="stonetop-journey{{#if journey.needsStart}} is-needing-start{{/if}}">

  {{> "stonetop.expedition-journey-controls" journey=journey}}

  {{#if journey.map}}
  {{!-- The wrapper exists for the expand button and for nothing else: it is what that button's
        corner is measured from. That button is a SIBLING of the map box rather than a child of it
        because the box is what the draw and place-a-site gestures listen on (ExpeditionDialog
        `_armPanelDrawing`, `_placeSiteFromPanel`) — inside it, pressing the button would be
        indistinguishable from clicking that spot on the map. --}}
  <div class="stonetop-journey-mapwrap">
    {{!-- aspect-ratio comes from the probed file, so the box has its true height before the image
          decodes and the hotspots land right on the first paint instead of jumping. --}}
    <div class="stonetop-journey-map stonetop-journey-canvas" style="aspect-ratio:{{journey.map.aspect}}">
      <img class="stonetop-journey-img" src="{{journey.map.src}}" alt="{{journey.map.alt}}" draggable="false">

      {{> "stonetop.expedition-journey-pins" journey.map}}
    </div>
    {{!-- OPEN THE BIG MAP. The panel's map is only as wide as the step column and the books print
          these at 300 dpi, so there has always been a way out to the shared pan/zoom window. This
          is that way, off a line of its own and onto the picture it opens (user, 2026-08-24): it
          fades in over the map, which is where the reader's pointer already is when they want more
          of it, and the step column gets a row back.

          KEEPS ITS CLASS AND ITS DATASET, which is why no JS moved with it: `bindJourneyControls`
          finds this button by `.stonetop-journey-zoom` anywhere under the root it is handed, and
          reads which map to open off `data-key`. --}}
    <button type="button" class="stonetop-journey-zoom"
            data-src="{{journey.map.src}}" data-key="{{journey.activeTier}}" data-title="{{journey.map.alt}}"
            data-tooltip="See the whole map" aria-label="See the whole map">
      <i class="fas fa-expand"></i>
    </button>
  </div>
  {{!-- What a shift-click on the picture above does, which is the one thing about this map no
        reader could guess. AGAINST THE PICTURE, and now directly under it: it is that picture's
        caption, and it used to have to sit ahead of the zoom pill so a line of small centred italic
        did not read as a caption for a left-aligned button. That pill has gone onto the map itself,
        so the caption has the space under the art to itself. Only where there IS a picture, too —
        an invitation to click a map this world has no copy of would be teaching a gesture with
        nowhere to make it. --}}
  {{> "stonetop.expedition-journey-drawhint" journey=journey}}
  {{else}}
  <p class="stonetop-journey-nomap">
    {{#if journey.hasAnyMap}}
    That map isn&rsquo;t in this world yet. Try the other one, or use the list below.
    {{else}}
    The books&rsquo; maps aren&rsquo;t in this world. They come with your rulebooks rather than with
    the system, so run <strong>Import Book Art</strong> to add them. The distances below work either way.
    {{/if}}
  </p>
  {{/if}}

  {{> "stonetop.expedition-journey-route" journey=journey}}

  <div class="stonetop-journey-list">
    {{!-- WHAT THE FOLDS BELOW ARE. Each one is headed with the name of a map the books print, which
          says everything to a reader with the rulebooks open and nothing at all to anyone else: a
          GM who has never seen the Vicinity has no way to tell whether it is a region, a road or a
          season. The header names the three folds and says what the times beside every place mean,
          once, instead of leaving eighteen unexplained figures down the right-hand edge.

          It reads off the START rather than saying "from Stonetop", because a trip can set out from
          anywhere at all now — any of the eighteen places the books letter, or any point on either
          map — and every time in the list is re-solved when it does. A time reckoned from a point
          the GM placed wears a tilde: it is the book's own printed roads plus one leg this system
          measured. See ExpeditionDialog `_timeLabel`.

          AND WITH NO START IT NAMES NOBODY, because there is nothing to name: a trip right-clicked
          back past its own start has nowhere to reckon from, so the times beside these places are
          blank and the heading says only what the list IS. The next click sets the start and every
          one of them fills in. --}}
    <div class="stonetop-journey-listhead">
      {{!-- NO GLYPH ON THIS ONE. It wore the load diamond for a while, borrowed off the step's
            other card heads (the Steading assets bar carries one) — but ◇ is this system's LOAD
            mark, and it means a weight of gear everywhere else it is printed. Over a list of
            travel times it is a mark the reader has been taught to read as something else. --}}
      <span class="stonetop-journey-listtitle">Travel times{{#if journey.start.set}} from {{journey.start.name}}{{/if}}</span>
      <span class="stonetop-journey-listnote">at least this long</span>
    </div>
    {{!-- What each fold is, and nothing about how to use the list: the line under the map already
          says what a click does, and saying it twice on one screen reads as two different
          instructions. --}}
    {{!-- WHAT A CLICK ON A ROW WILL DO, but only where there is no picture to say it instead. With
          a map on screen the caption under it already teaches the gesture, and two instructions
          about one screen read as two different ones. Without a map this list IS the screen, so
          this is the only place the state can be said at all — and it is the state the trip is in
          after a right-click has taken its start away. --}}
    {{#unless journey.map}}
    {{#if journey.needsStart}}
    <p class="stonetop-journey-listhint stonetop-journey-listhint--start">
      Nobody has said where they set out from yet. Tap a place to leave from there;
      right-click a place to take the trip back a step.
    </p>
    {{/if}}
    {{/unless}}
    <p class="stonetop-journey-listhint">
      Every place the books give a time for, filed under the map it is drawn on.
      <strong>The Vicinity</strong> is the country around Stonetop, reckoned in hours and days;
      <strong>The World&rsquo;s End</strong> is the wider region past it, reckoned in days;
      <strong>Beyond the maps</strong> is what lies off the edge of both.
    </p>
    {{#each journey.groups}}
    {{#if places.length}}
    <div class="stonetop-journey-group">
      <div class="stonetop-journey-grouphead">{{label}}</div>
      <ul>
        {{#each places}}
        {{!-- THE BOOK TRAILS THE NAME, at the far end of the row, which is where it has always
              been (user, 2026-08-24). It is what you reach for AFTER reading the place, so it
              follows the place. The gutter it sits in is still a fixed column rather than
              whatever space the name and time leave over, so the one place in eighteen the books
              give no entry keeps its column empty instead of letting its name run wide. --}}
        <li>
          <button type="button" data-slug="{{slug}}"
                  class="stonetop-journey-pick stonetop-journey-row{{#if isOrigin}} is-origin{{/if}}{{#if isChosen}} is-chosen{{/if}}">
            <span class="j-name">{{name}}</span>
            {{#if isOrigin}}<span class="j-here">setting out</span>{{else if time}}<span class="j-time">{{time}}</span>{{/if}}
          </button>
          {{#if uuid}}
          {{!-- `data-link` is what makes this open, NOT `.content-link`: core delegates its
                content-link click (and drag) off `a[data-link]` and only paints the class. An
                anchor with the class alone looks exactly like a link and does nothing. --}}
          <a class="content-link stonetop-journey-book" data-link draggable="true"
             data-uuid="{{uuid}}" data-tooltip="Read the gazetteer">
            <i class="fas fa-book-open"></i>
          </a>
          {{/if}}
        </li>
        {{/each}}
      </ul>
    </div>
    {{/if}}
    {{/each}}
  </div>
</div>
